<article class="env-remark" id="030149">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/030149">Remark <span data-tag="030149">7.2.4.6</span>.</a> It is not necessarily true that a Heyting space which is a union of finitely many <em>locally closed</em> subsets of dimension $\leq \alpha $ is also of dimension $\leq \alpha $. For example, a topological space with $2$ points and a nondiscrete, nontrivial topology has Heyting dimension $1$, but is a union of two locally closed subsets of Heyting dimension $0$. </p>
